                                    <h2><span class="shaft"><img id="1687-0" src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/icons/patterns/icon_8.png" width=27 height=27 alt="Bidirectional" title="Bidirectional" ></span>AEA N8</h2>
                    
                    <h2 class="sub">                    Bidirectional Active Ribbon Microphone</h2>
                    <p>The N8 is the second active ribbon mic in AEA&#8217;s &#8220;Nuvo&#8221; series, following the N22. </p><p>The N8 shares the N22&#8217;s body and grille construction, although it has a black rather than silver finish. The N8 also shares the N22&#8217;s ribbon dimensions, transformer, and active transformerless electronics. </p><p>These mics&#8217; electronics operate at unity gain; the output signal from the ribbon is amplified by a Lehle step-up transformer. The circuitry following the transformer serves to lower the output impedance to 92&Omega; without applying additional gain.</p><p>The ribbon in both Nuvo mics measures roughly 4.7mm x 60mm, and is made from corrugated 1.8-micron aluminum. But these identical ribbons are built into different motor designs.</p><p>While the N22 used multiple layers of acoustic filter materials to reduce bass response, the N8 has a fuller low frequency response, and appears to have a more traditional ribbon voicing, with a fuller low-frequency response. </p><p>Unfortunately, AEA&#8217;s limited test facilities do not show either mic&#8217;s low-frequency response, as they are truncated at 200Hz. But even at 200Hz, the N22 is beginning to roll off, while the N8 shows a slight increase in its response.<p>Permalink: <a href="//recordinghacks.com/microphones/AEA/N8">AEA N8</a></p><p>The mic was released in 2014.</p>
